    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID55",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q7d6321c22eeaf2fc-chunk_371445a7ea454e971118f066",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.3 dehumanising attacks and overt sexual objectification</strong> – Derogating women by comparing them to non-human entities such as vermin, disease or refuse, or overtly reducing them to sexual objects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">1.2. Defining cyber violence and hate speech online against women > 1.2.2. Academic and civil society definitions > Sexist hate speech<br><br>Sexist hate speech is defined as expressions which spread, incite, promote or justify hatred based on sex.\n\n<sup>31</sup> This section builds on the definitions presented earlier in the study and draws on definitions proposed in the following reports: Internet Governance Forum (2015), “Online Abuse and Gender-Based Violence Against Women”, available at https://www.intgovforum.org/multilingual/content/online-abuse-and-gender-based-violence-against-women, as well as the database produced by the Women’s Media Center, “Online Abuse 101”, available at http://www.womensmediacenter.com/speech-project/online-abuse-101\n____________________________________________________________________________\n\nPosting and sharing violent content consist of portraying women as sexual objects or targets of violence.\n\nUse of sexist and insulting comments, abusing women for expressing their own views and for turning away sexual advances.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q7d6321c22eeaf2fc-chunk_371445a7ea454e971118f066",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
