    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID73",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q4bb98986f215f3b2-chunk_9f165f40f35ffb5da27c3f4c",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.4 condescending explanations or unwelcome advice</strong> – Offering unsolicited or patronising advice to women on topics and issues they know more about (known as ‘mansplaining’).</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Subordination and trivialisation: Patronising language > Gender-sensitive language<br><br>> Tip!\n>\n> Think about the meaning of the words kitchenette and novelette compared to the words kitchen and novel to see the effect of the -ette suffix.\n\nLanguage which refers to people unknown to you in terms of endearment (‘My dear’, ‘Darling’, ‘Love’, and ‘Dear’ when used in speech) is patronising, condescending and promotes trivialisation. These forms should not be used unless the interlocutor has a close relationship with the speaker.\n\nAnother common way of trivialising women is to refer to adult women as ‘girls’. This is patronising and should be avoided.\n\nAdditionally, sometimes the word ‘woman’ is used dismissively e.g. ‘Get out of my way, woman!’\n\nAs well as avoiding obvious gender insults you should take care to avoid patronising women using more innocuous terms.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q4bb98986f215f3b2-chunk_9f165f40f35ffb5da27c3f4c",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
